# WCAG Guideline 1: Perceivable

The first WCAG principle is **perceivable**. The standard phrases it as: information and user interface components must be presentable to users in ways they can perceive. In other words, every user needs a way to take in the information your page presents, whether through sight, sound, or assistive technology such as a screen reader.

Common ways to satisfy this principle:

- provide text alternatives for non-text content (for example, `alt` text on images and captions or transcripts for video),
- do not rely on color alone to convey meaning,
- keep enough contrast between text and its background,
- let content adapt (resize, reflow) without losing meaning.

## Text Alternatives

Images that communicate information need useful `alt` text so that people who cannot see the image still receive the same meaning.

```{html}
<img src="chart.png" alt="Bar chart showing sales increasing from January to March">
```

- The `alt` text describes the meaning of the image, not the fact that it is an image. Avoid writing `alt="image"` or `alt="chart.png"`.
- A screen reader announces the `alt` text in place of the picture, so it should carry the same information a sighted user gets from looking at the chart.
- Videos and audio need their own alternatives: captions for people who cannot hear the audio, and transcripts so the content can be read.

## Do Not Rely on Color Alone

Consider a restaurant menu where a legend says "red means spicy" and some dishes are shown in red. A sighted user can see which dishes are red, but a screen reader cannot announce a color, so that user never learns which dishes are spicy. This violates **success criterion 1.4.1**: color is not used as the only visual means of conveying information, indicating an action, or distinguishing an element.

The same problem shows up in data visualizations that tell two series apart using only red and green. Beyond the screen-reader issue, red and green are a poor choice because a significant portion of the population is red-green colorblind, and to them the two series look nearly identical.

The fix is to pair color with a second cue, such as text, an icon, or a pattern:

```html
<!-- Color alone: meaning is lost without sight -->
<span style="color: red">Vindaloo</span>

<!-- Color plus a text label: everyone gets the meaning -->
<span style="color: red">Vindaloo</span> <span class="tag">(spicy)</span>
```

## Color Contrast

Even when color is not the only cue, text still has to be readable against its background. **Success criterion 1.4.3** requires a contrast ratio of at least **4.5:1** between text and its background (with a few exceptions, such as large text). Combinations like red text on a blue background, or light gray text on white, fall below that ratio and are hard to read.

To check a pair of colors, use an online tool such as the [WebAIM Contrast Checker](https://webaim.org/resources/contrastchecker/): enter a foreground and a background color and it reports the ratio and whether it passes the WCAG levels.

One caveat: passing the 4.5:1 ratio does not guarantee the colors are pleasant to read. Two highly saturated colors, such as bright green text on a bright blue background, can clear the contrast threshold and still be uncomfortable, so use the checker as a floor, not a guarantee.

## Exercises

1. Add meaningful alt text to the image. Use the exact text `Line chart of weekly signups`.

   ```{html}
   <img src="signups.png" alt="">
   ```
   ```+solution
   <img src="signups.png" alt="Line chart of weekly signups">
   ```
   ```+test
   wrap(source.includes('alt="Line chart of weekly signups"') || source.includes("alt='Line chart of weekly signups'")).should('equal', true).run('Add the exact alt text', 'Added meaningful alt text');
   ```

2. Imagine the notice below were shown in a light gray such as `#eee` on a white background, which would fail the 4.5:1 contrast ratio. Style `.notice` to give it `black` text on a `white` background so it comfortably passes.

   ```html id=page1
   <p class="notice">Kitchen closes at 9pm</p>
   ```

   ```{css}
   /* Write your CSS here */
   ```
   ```+solution
   .notice {
     color: black;
     background-color: white;
   }
   ```
   ```+id=page1
   ```
   ```+test
   assert('.notice').should('exist').should('have.css', 'color: black').run('Set the notice text color to black', 'Set readable black text');
   assert('.notice').should('have.css', 'background-color: white').run('Set the notice background to white', 'Set a white background');
   ```

3. Why is showing spicy dishes only in red text a problem?

   ```{mcq}
   Who misses the information, and why?
   correct: Correct. Color alone is not conveyed to screen readers, and red-green colorblind users may not perceive the difference either. This violates success criterion 1.4.1.
   wrong: Think about screen-reader users and red-green colorblind users, who cannot rely on the color.
   [x] Color alone does not reach screen readers, and colorblind users may not perceive it, so the meaning is lost.
   [ ] Red text is not allowed anywhere on the web.
   [ ] CSS cannot apply the color red to text.
   [ ] Screen readers refuse to read any styled text.
   ```

4. What is the minimum contrast ratio that success criterion 1.4.3 requires for standard-sized text at Level AA?

   ```{mcq}
   How much contrast does normal text need?
   correct: Correct! 4.5:1 for standard-sized text, with an allowance for large text. A ratio of 1:1 is invisible and 21:1 is black on white, the maximum possible.
   wrong: One of these is the maximum possible ratio and one is no contrast at all. The requirement is between them.
   [x] 4.5:1
   [ ] 1:1
   [ ] 3:1
   [ ] 21:1
   ```

